  <!-- LOCAL KNOWLEDGE SECTION -->
  <section class="pf-section" style="background:#f8fafc;">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="pf-section-label">NORRIS GREEN LOCAL KNOWLEDGE</span>
      <h2>The L11 Housing Story Most Plumbers Miss</h2>

      <div class="pf-card pf-card-left-orange" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
        <p>Norris Green was built from 1925 as one of Europe's biggest planned council estates. Liverpool City Council bought the land from Lord Derby's estate for £65,000. The plan was to rehouse families from the slum terraces in the inner city.</p>

        <p>The estate boomed. 25,000 people moved in inside three years. The population peaked above 37,000. The art deco library on Broadway opened in the 1930s and still stands today.</p>

        <p>Most of the houses are solid brick semis. Three bedrooms, family layouts, big enough to be a forever home. These are the ones still going strong today. Our regular L11 work is on this stock.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pf-card pf-card-left-orange" style="margin-top:1rem;">
        <p>A smaller chunk of the estate was built with the Boot system. Precast concrete columns with steel rebar inside. The rebar rusted over time. The columns cracked. By the 1980s these homes were unfit to live in.</p>

        <p>The fix took 20 years. 835 Boot homes were demolished between 2000 and 2006. Countryside Properties and Sigma started the rebuild in 2011. Norris Green Village is now 829 new homes on that cleared land. Modern plumbing, modern boilers, mostly still under warranty.</p>

        <p>That mix is the L11 reality. A 1930s semi on one street, a 2018 new build round the corner, a 2012 refurb across the road. We work on all of them at the same fixed rates.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


